  "sections": [
    {
      "agent": "web_search_agent",
      "subtopic": "Solar Energy (Photovoltaic and Thermal)",
      "findings": [
        {
          "fact": "Photovoltaic (PV) cells convert sunlight directly into electricity through the photovoltaic effect, while solar thermal systems use the sun's heat to warm water or air for heating purposes.",
          "source_url": "https://www.energy.gov/eere/solar",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "As of 2024, solar energy accounts for approximately 4-5% of global electricity generation, with rapid growth rates of 15-25% annually in recent years.",
          "source_url": "https://www.iea.org/reports/solar-pv-global-supply-chains",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "The levelized cost of electricity (LCOE) from utility-scale solar PV has declined by approximately 89% between 2010 and 2020, making it one of the cheapest electricity sources.",
          "source_url": "https://www.irena.org/publications/2021-Jun/Renewable-Cost-Database",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "Silicon-based solar cells dominate the global PV market, accounting for approximately 95% of all commercial solar panels, with monocrystalline and polycrystalline variants being most common.",
          "source_url": "https://www.iea.org/reports/solar-pv-global-supply-chains",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "Solar thermal systems can achieve efficiency rates of 50-80% in converting solar radiation to usable heat, while photovoltaic systems typically achieve 15-22% electrical conversion efficiency in commercial applications.",
          "source_url": "https://www.energy.gov/eere/solar",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "China leads global solar PV capacity installation with over 400 GW installed as of 2023, followed by the European Union, United States, Japan, and India.",
          "source_url": "https://www.iea.org/statistics/electricity",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "Emerging PV technologies including perovskite solar cells, tandem solar cells, and thin-film technologies are in development stages with potential efficiencies exceeding 30% in laboratory conditions.",
          "source_url": "https://www.nrel.gov/pv/cell-efficiency.html",
          "confidence": "medium"
        },
        {
          "fact": "Solar thermal systems are widely used for residential water heating, space heating, and industrial process heat applications, with significant deployment in countries like Austria, Germany, and Australia.",
          "source_url": "https://www.solarthermalworld.org/",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "Concentrated Solar Power (CSP) systems use mirrors to concentrate sunlight and generate heat for electricity production, with some installations featuring thermal storage capabilities allowing energy generation after sunset.",
          "source_url": "https://www.energy.gov/eere/solar",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "The International Energy Agency projects that solar will be the world's leading source of electricity by 2050, requiring cumulative investments of approximately $4 trillion through 2050.",
          "source_url": "https://www.iea.org/reports/solar-pv-global-supply-chains",
          "confidence": "medium"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "agent": "doc_analysis_agent",
      "subtopic": "Wind Energy (Onshore and Offshore)",
      "findings": [
        {
          "fact": "Wind energy is the second-largest source of renewable electricity globally, accounting for approximately 7-8% of worldwide electricity generation as of 2023, with continued growth in capacity.",
          "source_url": "https://www.iea.org/reports/renewables-2023",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "Onshore wind turbines typically have capacity factors ranging from 35-45%, while offshore wind turbines achieve higher capacity factors of 50-60% due to stronger and more consistent wind resources.",
          "source_url": "https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/79752.pdf",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "Modern onshore wind turbines have rotor diameters of 100-220 meters and hub heights of 80-180 meters, while offshore turbines are significantly larger with capacities reaching 12-15 MW per unit.",
          "source_url": "https://www.windeurope.org/wp-content/uploads/files/about-wind/reports/WindEurope-Global-Offshore-Wind-Report-2024.pdf",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "The levelized cost of electricity (LCOE) for onshore wind has decreased by approximately 70% over the past decade, making it one of the cheapest electricity sources, while offshore wind costs have decreased by 50% since 2012.",
          "source_url": "https://www.irena.org/publications/2022-Jun/Renewable-Power-Generation-Costs-in-2022",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "Offshore wind farms require specialized installation vessels and subsea cable systems, with typical water depths for development ranging from 30-60 meters for current projects, though floating offshore platforms enable deployment in deeper waters (100+ meters).",
          "source_url": "https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy20osti/77235.pdf",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "Wind turbines have operational lifespans of 20-25 years, with recent advances in maintenance and refurbishment extending some projects to 30+ years of operation.",
          "source_url": "https://www.energy.gov/eere/wind-research",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "The primary environmental concerns for onshore wind include avian and bat mortality, with estimates of 140,000-500,000 bird deaths annually in the United States, though this remains lower than mortality from other human causes.",
          "source_url": "https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/how-many-birds-are-killed-wind-turbines-united-states",
          "confidence": "medium"
        },
        {
          "fact": "Offshore wind environmental impacts include potential effects on marine ecosystems, bird and bat populations, underwater noise from pile-driving during construction, and electromagnetic fields from subsea cables.",
          "source_url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fmars.2021.607098/full",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "Global offshore wind capacity reached approximately 56 GW by end of 2023, with Europe leading in deployment followed by China and emerging projects in Asia and North America.",
          "source_url": "https://gwec.net/global-wind-report-2024/",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "Wind resource assessment requires analysis of wind speed variability, turbulence, wind shear, and terrain effects, typically involving 12-24 months of on-site wind measurement data before project development.",
          "source_url": "https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/79752.pdf",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "Grid integration challenges for high wind penetration include variability of wind generation, the need for forecasting systems, and requirements for grid flexibility, energy storage, or demand response mechanisms.",
          "source_url": "https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21nrel/79755.pdf",
          "confidence": "high"
        },
        {
          "fact": "Wind turbine blade materials have evolved from fiberglass to advanced composites including carbon fiber and hybrid materials, with blade lengths now exceeding 100 meters requiring new manufacturing and transportation techniques.",
          "source_url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1359836821002225",
          "confidence": "medium"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
