      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Concentrated Solar Power (CSP)\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Concentrated Solar Power uses mirrors or lenses to concentrate sunlight onto a small area to generate heat, which can be converted to electricity through a heat engine or stored for later use\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy14/61141.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"CSP systems typically require direct normal irradiance (DNI) exceeding 2000 kWh/m\u00b2/year to be economically viable, making them most suitable for arid and semi-arid regions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2020/Dec/Renewable-Capacity-Statistics-2020\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Main CSP technologies include parabolic troughs, solar towers (central receivers), linear Fresnel reflectors, and dish-Stirling systems\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy14/61141.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"CSP plants can integrate thermal energy storage systems (typically molten salt) to store heat and generate electricity during cloudy periods or at night, providing dispatchable renewable power\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/solar/concentrating-solar-power\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Parabolic trough systems are the most commercially deployed CSP technology, representing approximately 70% of global CSP capacity as of 2020\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2021/Jun/Renewable-Capacity-Statistics-2021\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Global CSP capacity was approximately 6.2 GW as of 2021, with Spain and the United States accounting for the majority of installed systems\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2022/Apr/Renewable-Capacity-Statistics-2022\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"CSP systems typically operate at thermal efficiencies of 20-35%, with overall system efficiencies ranging from 15-25% when accounting for conversion and storage losses\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy14/61141.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The levelized cost of electricity (LCOE) from CSP with thermal storage has declined by approximately 50% between 2010 and 2020\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2020/Dec/Renewable-Cost-and-Competitiveness\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"CSP requires significant water consumption for cooling and mirror cleaning, which can be a constraint in arid regions where most CSP plants are located\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy14/61141.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Recent CSP development has focused on increasing operating temperatures (approaching 700\u00b0C) to improve efficiency and reduce costs through better integration with thermal storage\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/solar/concentrating-solar-power\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
