      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Hydroelectric and Hydrokinetic Power\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydropower is the world's largest source of renewable electricity, accounting for approximately 16% of global electricity generation as of 2023\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/energy-system/electricity/hydropower\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydroelectric dams can have operational lifespans of 50-100+ years with minimal fuel costs once constructed\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/how-long-do-hydroelectric-dams-last\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydrokinetic energy (tidal and wave power) is still in early development stages with limited commercial deployment compared to traditional hydroelectric power\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/water-power/marine-hydrokinetic-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Run-of-river hydroelectric systems generate power without large water storage reservoirs, reducing environmental impact compared to traditional dams\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.waterpower.org/what-is-hydropower/types-of-hydropower\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tidal energy systems can generate electricity with predictability advantages over wind and solar due to lunar cycles that can be predicted years in advance\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/water-power/tidal-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wave energy conversion technologies include point absorbers, attenuators, and terminator devices, with most systems still in prototype or pilot stages\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/water/wave-energy.html\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The U.S. generates approximately 6% of its electricity from hydropower, with the Pacific Northwest region producing over 50% of national hydroelectric output\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/hydropower/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Pumped-storage hydroelectric systems can store energy by pumping water uphill during low-demand periods and releasing it during peak demand\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/pumped-storage-hydroelectricity\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydroelectric power plants have the ability to quickly adjust output in response to demand changes, making them valuable for grid stability and balancing variable renewables\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/hydropower-can-help-integrate-more-renewables\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Environmental concerns with large hydroelectric projects include fish migration disruption, methane emissions from reservoirs in tropical regions, and ecosystem alteration\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.worldwildlife.org/threats/dams-and-river-fragmentation\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
