      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Fusion Energy (Experimental)\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The National Ignition Facility (NIF) achieved fusion ignition on December 5, 2022, producing more energy output than was directly delivered to the fuel, marking the first time net energy gain has been demonstrated in a controlled fusion experiment.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/articles/national-ignition-facility-achieves-fusion-ignition\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"ITER (International Thermonuclear Experimental Reactor) in France is under construction and aims to demonstrate that fusion can be sustained and controlled, with planned operations beginning in the late 2020s.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iter.org/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Commonwealth Fusion Systems is developing the SPARC tokamak reactor, which uses high-temperature superconducting magnets and is expected to begin operations in the mid-2020s as a commercially-focused demonstration plant.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.commonwealthfusion.com/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"China's Artificial Sun (EAST reactor) achieved a plasma temperature of 120 million degrees Celsius in 2021, sustaining the reaction for over 100 seconds, advancing inertial confinement fusion research.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://english.cas.cn/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Private fusion companies including TAE Technologies, Helion Energy, and Type One Energy are pursuing alternative fusion approaches including field-reversed configurations and stellarators with venture capital funding exceeding $5 billion globally as of 2023.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.mckinsey.com/industries/electric-power-and-natural-gas/our-insights/private-fusion-energy-companies\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Inertial confinement fusion using lasers requires achieving specific conditions of temperature, density, and confinement time, with NIF using 192 laser beams to compress fuel pellets containing deuterium and tritium isotopes.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.llnl.gov/news/national-ignition-facility-achieves-fusion-ignition\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Magnetic confinement fusion designs like tokamaks and stellarators use powerful magnetic fields to confine plasma at temperatures exceeding 100 million degrees Celsius for sustained fusion reactions.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explained-fusion-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The U.S. Department of Energy has prioritized fusion energy development and established the Fusion Energy Sciences program, with increased federal funding allocated in recent fiscal years to advance experimental fusion technologies.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/science/fes/fusion-energy-sciences\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
