      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Airborne Wind Energy Systems\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Airborne wind energy (AWE) systems use tethered aircraft, kites, or balloons at high altitudes to capture wind energy and convert it to electricity, operating at heights where wind resources are stronger and more consistent than at ground level.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy13tos_field.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Makani, a company owned by Alphabet, developed an autonomous energy kite system that generated electricity by flying figure-eight patterns at altitudes of 300-500 meters before the company shut down in 2020.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://makani.withgoogle.com/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Companies such as Windlift, SkySails, and Altaeros Energies are actively developing airborne wind energy systems with different designs including rigid wings, flexible kites, and tethered balloons.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ise.fraunhofer.de/en/research-areas/renewable-energy-systems.html\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"AWE systems have theoretical advantages including access to stronger winds at altitude (wind power increases with height following wind shear), lower material costs compared to traditional wind turbines, and the ability to operate in locations where conventional turbines cannot be installed.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0960148121014215\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Technical challenges for airborne wind energy systems include control and stability of tethered aircraft, energy losses in tether systems, reliability of continuous operation, regulatory airspace concerns, and the need for advanced materials and power conversion systems.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy20osti/77040.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The global airborne wind energy market is projected to grow significantly, with estimates suggesting the technology could represent a meaningful portion of renewable energy generation by 2050 if technical and regulatory hurdles are overcome.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.marketresearchfuture.com/reports/airborne-wind-energy-market\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The International Energy Agency and various research institutions including Fraunhofer and NREL have identified airborne wind energy as a promising technology that could complement conventional wind and solar energy systems.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/wind-energy-portfolio-perspectives\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
