      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Hydrogen Fuel Production and Fuel Cells\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydrogen can be produced through several methods including steam methane reforming (most common, 95% of current production), electrolysis of water, and biomass gasification\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydrogen-fuel-cells\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Green hydrogen is produced using renewable electricity via electrolysis, while grey hydrogen comes from fossil fuels and blue hydrogen involves carbon capture from grey hydrogen production\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/energy-system/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Fuel cells generate electricity through an electrochemical reaction between hydrogen and oxygen, producing water as the only emission\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.fuelcellsworks.com/fuel-cells/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Proton Exchange Membrane (PEM) fuel cells are most commonly used for transportation applications, while Solid Oxide Fuel Cells (SOFCs) are used for stationary power generation\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydrogen-fuel-cells\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"As of 2023, electrolyzer costs have decreased significantly, with predictions that green hydrogen production costs could reach $1-2 per kilogram by 2030 in optimal conditions\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/energy-system/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydrogen fuel cell vehicles have driving ranges comparable to conventional vehicles (300-500+ miles) and refueling times of 3-5 minutes\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydrogen-fuel-cells\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Current global hydrogen production is approximately 94 million tonnes annually, with less than 1% from renewable sources as of 2023\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/energy-system/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Major industrial applications for hydrogen include ammonia synthesis, oil refining, and methanol production, accounting for over 70% of current hydrogen demand\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/energy-system/hydrogen\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The round-trip efficiency of hydrogen production via electrolysis and fuel cell conversion is approximately 35-50%, compared to 85-90% for rechargeable batteries\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydrogen-fuel-cells\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Hydrogen storage challenges include volume requirements, with current best-case storage at 6% hydrogen by weight in liquid form, necessitating dedicated infrastructure development\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/hydrogen-fuel-cells\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
