  "content": [
    {
      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Tidal and Wave Energy\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tidal energy is highly predictable and consistent, with tidal cycles occurring twice daily and being accurately forecastable years in advance, making it one of the most reliable renewable energy sources available.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2020/Dec/Ocean-Energy-Technology-Brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wave energy has a much higher energy density than wind or solar power, with wave power density typically ranging from 2-4 kW/m of wave front in moderate sea states, making it an attractive energy source.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/water-power/wave-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tidal stream turbines operate similarly to wind turbines but are submerged underwater, with typical rotor diameters of 15-20 meters and rated capacities of 1-2 MW per unit.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2014/Nov/Ocean-Energy-Technology-Brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wave energy converters use various technologies including point absorbers, attenuators, oscillating water columns, and overtopping devices to capture energy from ocean waves.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy10a/46034.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Global installed capacity of tidal energy as of 2023 is approximately 600 MW, with significant projects in South Korea, Canada, France, and the United Kingdom.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/statistics\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wave energy technology remains largely in demonstration and pre-commercial stages, with global installed capacity below 20 MW as of 2023, representing a much earlier development stage than tidal energy.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ocean-energy-systems.org/oes-reports/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The Rance Tidal Power Station in France, operational since 1966, remains one of the largest tidal barrages in the world with a 240 MW capacity and demonstrates the long-term viability of tidal energy.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.world-nuclear.org/information-library/current-and-future-generation/tidal-power.aspx\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Environmental concerns for tidal and wave energy include potential impacts on marine life from moving turbine blades, electromagnetic fields from subsea cables, habitat alteration, and acoustic effects.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iucn.org/resources/issues-briefs/ocean-energy-and-biodiversity\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tidal energy has capacity factors typically ranging from 35-50%, significantly higher than wind (25-35%) and solar (15-25%), indicating better utilization of installed capacity.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2020/Dec/Ocean-Energy-Technology-Brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wave energy resource is seasonally variable, with highest energy availability during winter months in most mid-to-high latitude locations, presenting challenges for consistent power generation.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/water-power/wave-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
