{
  "id": "msg_011Cf7asHzb9uZmPUAtYDqNc",
  "container": null,
  "content": [
    {
      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Emerging Technologies (Piezoelectric, Thermoelectric, etc.)\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Piezoelectric materials generate electrical charge when mechanically stressed, and can be applied in energy harvesting from vibrations, mechanical deformation, and human motion in wearable devices and structural monitoring systems.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/nmat2921\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Thermoelectric devices exploit the Seebeck effect to directly convert heat differentials into electrical voltage, with potential applications in waste heat recovery from industrial processes and vehicle exhaust.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/nmat1861\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Lead telluride (PbTe) and bismuth telluride (Bi2Te3) are currently among the most commercially viable thermoelectric materials, with figure of merit (ZT) values reaching approximately 1.0-1.5 at specific temperatures.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1126/science.283.5403.804\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Piezoelectric energy harvesting can generate power densities of 0.01-1 mW/cm\u00b3 depending on material composition and stress frequency, though currently insufficient for most high-power applications without supplementary energy sources.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1039/b904382g\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Organic thermoelectric materials and hybrid perovskites represent emerging research areas with potential for lower cost and greater flexibility compared to inorganic alternatives, though current ZT values remain below 1.0 for most organic systems.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/nmat4676\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Nanotechnology approaches including nanowires, nanostructured composites, and quantum confinement effects have shown promise in improving ZT values of thermoelectric materials by reducing thermal conductivity while maintaining electrical conductivity.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/nature04725\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Pyroelectric materials can convert thermal fluctuations into electrical energy and represent an emerging technology category distinct from thermoelectrics, with potential for harvesting from ambient temperature variations.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/nmat3372\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Tri-generation systems combining thermoelectric, piezoelectric, and photovoltaic harvesting represent a research frontier, though practical integration and device efficiency optimization remain ongoing challenges.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rser.2015.01.012\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Current thermoelectric power generation systems achieve overall energy conversion efficiencies of 3-8% in practical applications, significantly lower than the Carnot efficiency limit, making industrial-scale deployment cost-prohibitive for many applications.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1038/nature12373\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Piezoelectric nanogenerators utilizing zinc oxide (ZnO) nanowires and other nanostructures have demonstrated capacity for self-powered nanosensors and microdevices, though scaling to grid-relevant power levels remains theoretical.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1150243\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
