      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Geothermal Energy Systems\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Geothermal energy systems utilize heat from within the Earth to generate electricity or provide direct heating and cooling. The Earth's internal heat comes from radioactive decay of elements in the core and residual heat from planetary formation.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/faqs/what-geothermal-energy\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Traditional geothermal power plants require temperatures of at least 300\u00b0F (150\u00b0C) to generate electricity efficiently, typically found at depths of 1-3 km in geothermal resource areas.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/geothermal\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"There are three main types of geothermal power plants: dry steam plants, flash steam plants, and binary cycle plants, which differ in how they convert geothermal heat to electricity.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/geothermal/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Geothermal energy provides baseload power with capacity factors typically ranging from 70-90%, making it one of the most reliable renewable energy sources available.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/79236.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Global geothermal electricity generation capacity was approximately 14 GW as of 2020, with Indonesia, Philippines, Turkey, New Zealand, and Mexico being the leading producers.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2021-Apr/Renewable-Energy-Statistics-2021\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Enhanced Geothermal Systems (EGS) or engineered geothermal systems represent an emerging technology that creates reservoirs in hot rock formations where no natural permeability exists, potentially expanding geothermal development beyond traditional geothermal zones.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/geothermal/enhanced-geothermal-systems\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Direct-use applications of geothermal energy include district heating, greenhouse agriculture, aquaculture, industrial processes, and heat pump systems, which collectively account for significant energy utilization in some countries.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2022-Jun/Geothermal-Energy-Technology-Brief\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The levelized cost of electricity (LCOE) from geothermal plants ranges from $60-$100 per MWh for conventional geothermal resources, competitive with other renewable and conventional energy sources.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.lazard.com/perspective/levelized-cost-of-energy-and-levelized-cost-of-storage-2021/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Geothermal energy systems have minimal land use requirements per unit of energy produced compared to solar or wind farms, typically requiring 1-8 acres per megawatt.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy13osti/56290.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Environmental considerations for geothermal systems include potential induced seismicity from fluid injection, management of geothermal brines containing dissolved minerals, and localized land subsidence in some cases.\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usgs.gov/programs/VHP/volcanic-and-geothermal-hazards\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
  ],
