      "citations": null,
      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Carbon Capture and Utilization\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Carbon capture and utilization (CCU) technologies encompass processes that capture CO2 from industrial sources, the atmosphere, or directly from air, and convert it into useful products such as chemicals, building materials, fuels, and beverages\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/carbon-capture-utilisation-and-storage\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Point-source carbon capture from industrial facilities like cement, steel, and power plants is currently more cost-effective and mature than direct air capture (DAC), with typical costs ranging from $50-100 per ton of CO2 for industrial sources versus $150-300+ per ton for DAC\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg3/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Global CO2 capture capacity reached approximately 42 million metric tons per year as of 2023, with the majority used for enhanced oil recovery, though utilization in chemicals and building materials is growing\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.globalccsinstitute.com/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"CCU can be integrated with renewable energy systems to produce synthetic fuels and chemicals, enabling energy storage and reducing emissions from hard-to-abate sectors like aviation and heavy industry\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Current CCU utilization pathways include production of chemicals (methanol, urea), building materials (concrete, aggregates), beverages, polymers, and synthetic fuels, with chemical production representing the largest current market share\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/carbon-capture-utilisation-and-storage\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Policy support through carbon pricing, investment tax credits, and regulatory mandates is critical for CCU deployment, with policies like the U.S. 45Q tax credit significantly influencing project economics\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Technical challenges for scaling CCU include developing cost-competitive conversion technologies, achieving high energy efficiency conversion rates, and establishing sustainable markets for CCU-derived products\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg3/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Life cycle assessment studies indicate that CCU environmental benefits depend critically on the carbon intensity of energy sources used; renewable energy-powered CCU provides significantly greater net emissions reductions than fossil fuel-powered systems\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/journal/energy-conversion-and-management\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Emerging CCU technologies include electrochemical conversion, biological conversion using microorganisms and enzymes, and thermochemical processes, though most remain at pilot or demonstration scale\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/articles/carbon-capture-utilisation-and-storage\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The additionality of CCU as a climate solution is debated, as emissions reductions depend on whether captured CO2 replaces fossil carbon or represents net new CO2 removal, requiring careful accounting frameworks\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.ipcc.ch/report/ar6/wg3/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
