      "text": "```json\n{\n  \"subtopic\": \"Wind Energy (Onshore and Offshore)\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wind energy is the second-largest source of renewable electricity globally after hydropower, accounting for approximately 7-8% of global electricity generation as of 2023\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/wind-icr-2023\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Onshore wind turbines typically have capacity factors of 35-45%, while offshore wind turbines achieve capacity factors of 45-55% due to stronger and more consistent wind resources\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/80236.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Modern onshore wind turbines have rotor diameters of 120-220 meters and hub heights of 80-150 meters, with individual unit capacities ranging from 2.5-15 MW\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2022-Jun/Renewable-power-generation-costs-in-2021\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Offshore wind turbines are significantly larger, with capacities of 8-15 MW currently deployed and 20+ MW prototypes under development, featuring hub heights of 100-220 meters\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy22osti/83692.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"The levelized cost of electricity (LCOE) for onshore wind has declined by approximately 70% over the past decade (2010-2020), while offshore wind LCOE has decreased by approximately 50%\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2021-Jun/Renewable-Power-Generation-Costs-in-2020\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Onshore wind installations require approximately 5-10 acres per megawatt of capacity, while accounting for land-sharing possibilities with agriculture\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy16osti/65431.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Offshore wind farms are typically located 5-50 km from shore at depths of 5-50 meters for fixed-bottom installations and greater depths (50-1000+ meters) for floating systems\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/79408.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wind turbine blade materials have evolved from fiberglass to carbon-fiber composites, with blade recycling and circular economy approaches still in early development stages\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/articles/wind-turbine-blade-recycling-movement-gains-momentum\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Onshore wind has a capacity factor advantage in regions with consistent trade winds and mountain passes, while offshore wind is particularly viable in coastal regions with continental shelf access\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy21osti/78773.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Wind energy integration into electrical grids requires battery storage, demand response, and grid modernization to manage variability and intermittency challenges\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy20osti/76070.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Manufacturing and installation of wind turbines requires specialized infrastructure including ports for offshore equipment, specialized vessels, and trained workforce\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.iea.org/reports/offshore-wind-outlook-2023\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Environmental impacts of onshore wind include bird and bat mortality (estimated at 140,000-500,000 bird deaths annually in North America), while offshore wind impacts on marine ecosystems are still being researched\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.usbotc.org/documents/pdfs/whitepaper-avian-impacts.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Global wind energy capacity reached approximately 1,000 GW by 2023, with annual additions of 75-90 GW in recent years\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://gwec.net/global-wind-report-2023/\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"China, United States, Germany, India, and Spain are the leading countries in installed wind capacity, collectively accounting for over 70% of global capacity\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.irena.org/publications/2023-Mar/Renewable-Capacity-Statistics-2023\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fact\": \"Floating offshore wind technology enables deployment in deeper waters (200+ meters) and is expanding markets in Asia-Pacific and Northern Europe\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/docs/fy22osti/83692.pdf\",\n      \"confidence\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```",
      "type": "text"
    }
